#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121146 :tutorial/content "We can simplify the polynomial below because it contains a pair of like terms:\n${\\color{blue}{3x^2}} +4x \\,{\\color{blue}{-}}\\,{\\color{blue}{2x^2}} +5$\n\nTo simplify, we combine the like terms ($3x^2$ and $-2x^2$) by adding or subtracting their coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x^{2} + 4x - 2x^{2} + 5 &=  \\\\\n3x^{2} - 2x^{2} + 4x + 5 &=  \\\\\n(3x^{2} - 2x^{2}) + 4x + 5 &=  \\\\\n(3 - 2)x^{2} + 4x + 5 &=  \\\\\n&= x^{2} + 4x + 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dcac1959-d802-5641-8736-12d53db273a9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8864 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121147 :tutorial/content "To write a polynomial in **standard form**, we must arrange the terms of the variable's exponent in descending order. The first term should have the largest exponent, and the last term should have the smallest exponent.\n\nFor example, consider the following polynomial:\n$6x-2x^2-2+x^3$\n\nTo write this polynomial in standard form, we follow the steps below:\n\n1. Identify the term with the highest exponent ($x^3$) and write it first.\n2. Identify the term with the next highest exponent ($-2x^2$) and write it second.\n3. Continue identifying the term with the next highest exponent and writing it next until all of the polynomial terms have been written.\n\nTherefore, the standard form of our polynomial is\n$x^3 -2x^2 + 6x -2$.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The leading term always appears first when a polynomial is written in standard form. This is where the leading term gets its name!\n- When a polynomial is written in standard form, we also say that the terms are written in **decreasing powers of $\\boldsymbol x$** or **descending powers of $\\boldsymbol x$** because the powers are decreasing/descending.\n- If we were to write the terms of our polynomial in *increasing* (or *ascending*) powers of $x$, it would look as follows:\n$-2 + 6x -2x^2 + x^3$\nIn this case, the powers of $x$ *increase* as we read from left to right." :tutorial/id #uuid "40e8c8a9-715e-5a33-92e4-34369c27b505" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8868 :tutorial/title "Standard Form"}]}
